 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Rusting is the slow reaction of iron (or steel) with oxygen and water to form a reddish-brown substance called rust. Only iron and steel rust; other metals corrode.\nBoth air (oxygen) and water are needed. Experiment with three iron nails:\n• Tube A: nail in tap water open to air → it rusts.\n• Tube B: nail in boiled water with a layer of oil on top (no air) → no rust.\n• Tube C: nail in dry air with calcium chloride (a drying agent) → no rust.\nSalt water and acid rain make rusting faster. This is why cars and roofs near the sea, as in Limbe and Douala, rust quickly.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "t": "a nail in boiled water covered with oil"
  },
  "problem": "Will {t} rust?",
  "steps": [
   "Boiling removes the dissolved air, and the oil stops air entering.",
   "Water is present but oxygen is not."
  ],
  "answer": "No: rusting needs both oxygen and water.",
  "checks": []
 },
